WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:03.090
- The biggest fear I have right now is

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.550
in my popularity growth

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:05.700
that people are gonna try to be like me.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:06.559
I'm an enigma.

00:00:06.559 --> 00:00:10.752
I was in outer space, I
grew up in a very different,

00:00:10.752 --> 00:00:12.960
I was born in the old country.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.843
I'm more similar to
everybody's grandfather

00:00:14.843 --> 00:00:16.320
than I am to them.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:17.510
I'm a different animal.

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.595
Don't do it like me, don't do it like you,

00:00:19.595 --> 00:00:23.450
do it like us in our
buying into who we were

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:25.656
and how we could do it.

00:00:25.656 --> 00:00:27.250
(yelling)

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:28.753
You got your perspective.

00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:31.828
(audience applauding)

00:00:31.828 --> 00:00:34.913
I just wanna be happy,
don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:34.913 --> 00:00:38.100
(upbeat music)

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.068
Hey everybody, really excited
about today's podcast.

00:00:41.068 --> 00:00:42.360
Excited about the book,

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:43.680
but more excited about the gentleman

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.180
that's sitting in front of me.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.605
Doug is a an iconic, I like using that,

00:00:48.605 --> 00:00:52.700
iconic businessman and
CEO in the corporate world

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.810
and was at the helm of one of

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.970
VaynerMedia's earliest clients Campbell's

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.889
and an avid tennis fan,

00:00:59.889 --> 00:01:02.170
which is one of the first
things I think about with him

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:05.680
but somebody had stayed in
touch with over the last decade

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.180
who I have a lot of respect for

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.597
and when him and his team
reached out and said,

00:01:08.597 --> 00:01:10.127
"Hey, we've got a new book out,

00:01:10.127 --> 00:01:11.330
"we'd love to be on the podcast."

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.620
I couldn't have been more
excited for this moment

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.440
and so here we are.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.220
LinkedIn community I know
you're watching live.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.780
We're taking phone numbers on Facebook

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.120
where we're live as well.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.600
I'd really like to give
some of you access to Doug

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.623
because I think the
wisdom and the experience

00:01:25.623 --> 00:01:28.430
and "The Blueprint" that he brings

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:30.180
to the forefront is a valuable one

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.700
and I'm excited to give you context.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.440
I think a lot of times a
lot of the entrepreneurs

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.261
and executives from this
show get enormous value

00:01:36.261 --> 00:01:38.640
of some of the great's that have done it

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.650
and I feel like Doug sits in that category

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.285
and so I'm really excited
to see you my friend.

00:01:42.285 --> 00:01:43.925
- It's good to see you too.

00:01:43.925 --> 00:01:46.030
- Doug, why don't you give some context

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:47.666
'cause I do have an audience that I think

00:01:47.666 --> 00:01:49.730
we'll be hearing about
you for the first time

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:50.610
and I'm excited about that.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.034
So why don't you give a little
two to three minute rant,

00:01:53.034 --> 00:01:57.010
call it a comic book number
one origin story stuff.

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:58.693
You can go all the way back
to who you were as a kid

00:01:58.693 --> 00:02:00.090
or go into your professional stuff

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.344
but take the floor for three
to four minutes for context

00:02:01.344 --> 00:02:02.760
and we'll take it from there.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:05.491
- Sure, I grew up in a
very modest household

00:02:05.491 --> 00:02:07.740
outside of Chicago and--

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.311
- Were you a Bears fan?

00:02:09.311 --> 00:02:10.239
- Oh, big time.

00:02:10.239 --> 00:02:13.330
- Well Allen Robinson the star
wide receiver of the Bears,

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.410
literally just walked out
of here two seconds ago.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:16.960
- And I missed him.
- We gotta find him.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.100
Sheryl, go find him.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:18.933
We gotta get him for Doug.

00:02:18.933 --> 00:02:19.970
- If he's here, that'd be awesome.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:20.888
- [Gary] Yeah, it's done.

00:02:20.888 --> 00:02:21.721
We're gonna figure it out.

00:02:21.721 --> 00:02:23.440
I don't think he left
- We're on it man.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:24.950
- We're moving, improv-ing early.

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:26.130
Quick interrupts, just like I like it.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.243
- Now if you could get
Rizzo or Chris Bryant here

00:02:29.243 --> 00:02:30.538
that would be perfect.

00:02:30.538 --> 00:02:34.230
- Well speaking of Cubs,
sports cards are back

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.610
and I'm buying Javier Baez
rookie cards like crazy.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:37.978
- Oh yeah, yeah, I believe that.

00:02:37.978 --> 00:02:39.110
- [Gary] I think Javier's
a real player so go ahead.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:40.690
- All right.
- Outside of Chicago.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:42.180
- So I grew up in Chicago

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.390
and migrated to the corporate
world very early on.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:48.100
I tried to play professional tennis,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.140
that lasted a nanosecond

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:51.874
and then discovered I
couldn't make any money,

00:02:51.874 --> 00:02:53.459
went back finished my MBA,

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:55.800
went to work in the corporate world,

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.067
worked there for 40 years.
- It's unbelievable.

00:02:57.067 --> 00:03:00.660
- My first performance review
six months into my job,

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.260
you know your boss signs it

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:06.465
and your boss's boss had to read it

00:03:06.465 --> 00:03:08.320
and make a comment.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.512
My boss' boss on my first
performance review--

00:03:10.512 --> 00:03:12.310
- And real quick, this is
an exciting story already.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:13.980
Your six months in, where are you working,

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:14.813
what are you doing?

00:03:14.813 --> 00:03:16.550
- I'm working at General
Mills in Minneapolis--

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.961
- And you are a?
- I'm in brand management.

00:03:18.961 --> 00:03:22.280
A large consumer goods company.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:23.540
- What brand are you on?

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:28.540
- I was on Betty Crocker potato
buds and specialty potatoes.

00:03:28.777 --> 00:03:29.610
- [Gary] I love this stuff.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.980
- And then I moved on to
Betty Crocker brownies,

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:33.170
puddings and pie crusts.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:34.700
I did it all there.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.430
- You were a Betty Crocker juggernaut.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.790
- I was writing the
message on the back panel

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:40.753
from Betty Crocker--

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:43.080
- As Betty Crocker.
- As Betty Crocker.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.990
- So you are Betty Crocker at some point.

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:46.360
- I was, yeah.
- I love this.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.025
- My mother was in shock.

00:03:48.025 --> 00:03:49.890
She said, "What are you doing?"

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.177
I told her I'm writing
my Betty Crocker recipe.

00:03:52.177 --> 00:03:53.010
- I love it though,

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.250
so anyway six months in...

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:56.569
- Six months in I have my first review

00:03:56.569 --> 00:04:00.490
and my boss' boss, the
only line he writes is

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:02.218
you should be looking for another job.

00:04:02.218 --> 00:04:03.394
- [Gary] Really.

00:04:03.394 --> 00:04:06.760
- You know and I've moved
to Minneapolis, Minnesota,

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:09.930
don't know a soul and--

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:10.960
- It's cold as shit.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.136
- Yeah it is, it was March.

00:04:13.136 --> 00:04:15.920
The first 18 days I lived there

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.120
the high temperature was below zero.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:18.953
- [Gary] Unbelievable.

00:04:18.953 --> 00:04:21.360
- So he so it's below
zero, I'm getting feedback,

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.840
you should be looking for another job,

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:23.673
I don't know anybody.

00:04:23.673 --> 00:04:24.783
- How did you handle that?

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:28.190
- I did all right with it.

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:29.130
- [Gary] Good for you.

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:30.620
- You know you have two choices--

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.143
- [Gary] Fold or double down.

00:04:32.143 --> 00:04:33.967
- Yeah, fold or double
down and I doubled down

00:04:33.967 --> 00:04:36.330
and I was grinding it out.

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:37.793
- What did you learn from that?

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.890
What's the takeaway from that?

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:43.303
Was it about interactions with your boss,

00:04:44.268 --> 00:04:46.083
was there some other random insight?

00:04:47.602 --> 00:04:50.850
- What I learned from that probably was

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:52.733
that I could play through it.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.763
But early on I was like
a deer in the headlights.

00:04:56.763 --> 00:05:00.140
My first day at work I went
there off the tennis tour

00:05:02.909 --> 00:05:05.227
and everybody was wearing pinstripe suits,

00:05:05.227 --> 00:05:08.520
red ties, white shirts,
that was sort of the,

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:09.803
this was mid-70s.

00:05:10.752 --> 00:05:14.440
And I show up in a khaki suit
with a bright yellow shirt,

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:17.944
a big wide Madras tie
and brown Earth shoes

00:05:17.944 --> 00:05:21.497
and a tan line from where
I had my headband and--

00:05:21.497 --> 00:05:24.056
- Mustache?
- My Fu Manchu mustache.

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:24.889
- [Gary] Wow.

00:05:24.889 --> 00:05:26.600
- And here I'm showing up at

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.250
a button-down collar place and--

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:30.720
- Did anybody make a good funny comment

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:31.800
in the first 24 hours?

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.384
- No, but I--
- The looks.

00:05:33.384 --> 00:05:36.300
- I will tell you I started
to look like everybody else

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:37.400
about three months in.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.233
- [Gary] I understand.

00:05:38.233 --> 00:05:41.544
- I spent probably my entire time there,

00:05:41.544 --> 00:05:43.615
my focus was on fitting in

00:05:43.615 --> 00:05:47.990
because my feedback early on
was you're not fitting in.

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:52.070
So I was trying to figure
out how to make this work.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:53.810
It was a whole new world.

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:55.552
It'd be like walking in here
for the first time and--

00:05:55.552 --> 00:05:57.640
- Yes, the energy here
is a little different.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.640
- Wow, steroids.
- Yes.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.340
- But at any rate and
I was trying to fit in.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:03.173
- [Gary] Yes, I understand.

00:06:03.173 --> 00:06:04.067
- And I was trying to figure out--

00:06:04.067 --> 00:06:05.040
- Did you like the work?

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:06.638
I mean, obviously you stayed
in the industry for 40 years.

00:06:06.638 --> 00:06:08.998
- Well, I liked the people.
- You liked the people.

00:06:08.998 --> 00:06:10.530
- The work was fine--

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:11.690
- But you like the people part?

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.181
- It was interesting but the people--

00:06:13.181 --> 00:06:14.289
- [Gary] I like that.

00:06:14.289 --> 00:06:15.280
- And being part of a large community,

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:16.270
just like you've got here.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:17.103
- [Gary] Yeah.

00:06:17.103 --> 00:06:18.400
- Being part of a larger
community of people,

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.010
give each other energy
and if you needed help,

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:22.235
you found somebody to help you.

00:06:22.235 --> 00:06:24.010
- Did you like playing doubles

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:26.603
or in tennis you liked
more of a singles aspect?

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:29.203
- Both, both.

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:33.200
I was better at doubles but
if you're a tennis player

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:34.810
you're sort of a lone wolf anyway.

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:35.643
- [Gary] Yeah, yeah, that's right.

00:06:35.643 --> 00:06:37.133
- 'Cause it's not a team sport.

00:06:38.166 --> 00:06:39.913
You're playing for yourself.

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:42.656
- So what happened immediately after that?

00:06:42.656 --> 00:06:45.650
- Eventually I got fired from that job.

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:46.483
- [Gary] That's epic.

00:06:46.483 --> 00:06:48.783
- Nine years in, I went in, I was working,

00:06:48.783 --> 00:06:51.040
I had the world's greatest job.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:52.750
I was director of marketing

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:54.513
for Parker Brothers Toys and Games.

00:06:54.513 --> 00:06:55.530
- Oh.

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:57.872
- Nerf Sport, I had two little kids.

00:06:57.872 --> 00:06:59.600
Nerf Sports, Nerf football--

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.770
- The green and white Nerf football

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:03.500
is like the most Americana for me

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:05.190
because that's how I became a Jets fan.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:08.830
Like the Nerf spongy Nerf
green and white football

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:10.649
is like one of the most near
and dear things to my heart.

00:07:10.649 --> 00:07:12.284
- A lot of us grew up with 'em.

00:07:12.284 --> 00:07:13.117
- [Gary] Yep.

00:07:13.117 --> 00:07:13.950
- You got one right there.

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:14.783
- A little leather there

00:07:14.783 --> 00:07:15.616
but like that's what it looked like.

00:07:15.616 --> 00:07:17.500
- Well now you've gone upscale.

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.310
- Fair enough.
- But when you started.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:20.143
- Go buy me a Nerf football,

00:07:20.143 --> 00:07:21.431
I don't want to be known
as upscale, keep going.

00:07:21.431 --> 00:07:22.310
(laughing)

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:25.481
- Anyway, so I went in to work

00:07:25.481 --> 00:07:28.296
on a beautiful spring morning.

00:07:28.296 --> 00:07:29.650
- This is now nine years later.

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:31.760
- Nine years later up in Boston

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:34.247
and the receptionist said,

00:07:34.247 --> 00:07:35.417
"The senior vice president of marketing

00:07:35.417 --> 00:07:36.250
"would like to see you."

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:38.027
I go up to his office and he said,

00:07:38.027 --> 00:07:39.277
"Your job's been eliminated,

00:07:39.277 --> 00:07:41.330
"you'd need to be out of here by noon."

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:42.163
- Simple as that.

00:07:42.163 --> 00:07:44.410
- And nine years of my
career was over in a snap.

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:46.340
- So real quick, you were General Mills

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:47.173
for how long before went to Parker?

00:07:47.173 --> 00:07:48.820
- That was nine years.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:50.560
General Mills owned Parker Brothers.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:51.887
- I didn't know that at the time, got it.

00:07:51.887 --> 00:07:53.599
And later sold it to Hasbro?

00:07:53.599 --> 00:07:55.290
- Eventually yeah.
- Eventually.

00:07:55.290 --> 00:07:56.620
- Eventually they spun it off

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:57.830
and then Hasbro bought it.

00:07:57.830 --> 00:07:58.663
Yeah.

00:07:58.663 --> 00:08:00.890
But you know it was not easy.

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:01.760
- How old were you, 30?

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:03.070
- I was 32.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.019
- And what like crushed?

00:08:05.019 --> 00:08:06.380
That's a tough one.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:09.120
- That was the most
devastating day of my career.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:09.953
- No question.

00:08:09.953 --> 00:08:11.080
Did you have a mortgage?

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:13.230
- I had two kids, I had
a very large mortgage.

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:15.319
- [Gary] It's real stuff.

00:08:15.319 --> 00:08:16.573
- How am I gonna make this work?

00:08:16.573 --> 00:08:18.550
I had to go home and tell my wife.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:19.455
It was not pretty.

00:08:19.455 --> 00:08:22.400
- Not to completely go into that wound

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:24.060
but like you're a big
boy now and had a lot of,

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:25.290
wait till everybody hears.

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:26.900
He ended up being the CEO of Campbell's,

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:29.384
so like he won but like tell me about,

00:08:29.384 --> 00:08:31.150
tell me about that day.

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:32.280
Who'd you call first?

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:34.704
I'm actually very curious
about that, was it a parent?

00:08:34.704 --> 00:08:37.070
- I didn't call anybody,
you didn't know what to do.

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:38.310
- You were in a coma!
- I was told I needed

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:39.690
to be out of there by noon.

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:40.523
- [Gary] You cleaned your desk.

00:08:40.523 --> 00:08:42.280
- Yeah, clear your desk, put it in a box.

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.060
- [Gary] Did you talk to
anybody that you worked with?

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:44.893
- No.

00:08:44.893 --> 00:08:46.530
- [Gary] 'Cause you were in a daze.

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:48.520
- Yeah, I was absolutely in a daze.

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:49.968
- [Gary] Did you go to a bar right after?

00:08:49.968 --> 00:08:51.230
- I went straight home.

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:52.063
- [Gary] Okay fair enough.

00:08:52.063 --> 00:08:54.004
- Told to my wife and my two kids--

00:08:54.004 --> 00:08:54.837
- [Gary] How old were the kids?

00:08:54.837 --> 00:08:56.265
- The dogs, the cats.

00:08:56.265 --> 00:08:58.060
- [Gary] How did the cats take it?

00:08:58.060 --> 00:08:59.195
(laughing)

00:08:59.195 --> 00:09:01.696
- I don't know, I never
talked to them about it.

00:09:01.696 --> 00:09:02.670
- [Gary] Nonetheless.

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:03.760
- It was hard.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:04.593
- [Gary] What happened next?

00:09:04.593 --> 00:09:08.010
- They were so anxious to
get me out of the building

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.209
they forgot to tell me what my--

00:09:10.209 --> 00:09:11.584
- Package was.
- Benefits,

00:09:11.584 --> 00:09:13.300
what my package was.

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:16.430
So one of the HR people called me

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:18.163
and said I'd like to fill you in.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:21.191
And I swore at him

00:09:21.191 --> 00:09:22.620
and hung up the phone.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:24.586
You know, I was so pissed.

00:09:24.586 --> 00:09:26.230
It sort of all came out--

00:09:26.230 --> 00:09:27.830
- Your John McEnroe came out.

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:28.831
- Yeah, yeah there you go.

00:09:28.831 --> 00:09:29.710
- [Gary] And?

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:34.650
- And after about two more hours
with my kids running around

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:35.730
and the dogs and the cats.

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:37.430
I thought I need to get a job.

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:38.427
I better call that guy back.

00:09:38.427 --> 00:09:39.260
(laughing)

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:41.770
- Enough of this home life.

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:43.290
Two hours in.

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:46.130
- I tell you I called
him back that same day

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:48.880
and they sent me to an
outplacement counselor

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:50.858
who saved my life, saved my life.

00:09:50.858 --> 00:09:51.920
- [Gary] Interesting.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:55.440
- That same day, I had had the
worst experience of my career

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.795
and I mean that over my 45 year career

00:09:57.795 --> 00:10:01.500
and that afternoon and I call
my outplacement counselor guy

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:03.459
a guy by name of Neil McKenna,

00:10:03.459 --> 00:10:07.347
this is before cell phones
and before caller ID.

00:10:07.347 --> 00:10:10.297
He answers the phone, whoever
calls him and he says,

00:10:10.297 --> 00:10:12.617
"Hello, this is Neil
Mckenna, how can I help?"

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:15.470
And he was there for me completely.

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:17.027
I called him he said, "Come right on over,

00:10:17.027 --> 00:10:19.997
"we're gonna work through
this, we got to focus forward.

00:10:19.997 --> 00:10:23.117
"I know this is tough stuff."

00:10:24.827 --> 00:10:26.360
And he saved my life.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:27.193
- [Gary] Wow.

00:10:27.193 --> 00:10:29.021
- In that moment.

00:10:29.021 --> 00:10:31.330
And you know the funny thing was,

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:33.130
the first thing he had me do

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:35.610
and we talked about this right away.

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:38.683
He said, "I want you to hand write

00:10:38.683 --> 00:10:43.683
"your entire life story for
me and your family's story."

00:10:43.830 --> 00:10:45.640
And I'm thinking what
are you talking about?

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:46.675
I gotta get a job.

00:10:46.675 --> 00:10:47.971
- He wanted context.

00:10:47.971 --> 00:10:50.930
- And you want me to hand write,

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:52.511
he said "Yeah, I want your hand write it.

00:10:52.511 --> 00:10:55.327
"I want you to feel this,
hand write your life story.

00:10:55.327 --> 00:10:56.277
"Everything.

00:10:56.277 --> 00:10:58.940
"Everything you can think
of, leave nothing out."

00:10:58.940 --> 00:11:00.654
So I spent two weeks writing my life--

00:11:00.654 --> 00:11:01.487
(laughing)

00:11:01.487 --> 00:11:03.173
Two weeks writing my life story.

00:11:03.173 --> 00:11:04.884
- [Gary] No wonder you have a book.

00:11:04.884 --> 00:11:06.340
- When you're unemployed

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:07.430
you have a lot of time.
- You have time.

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:08.263
- You have a lot of time,

00:11:08.263 --> 00:11:10.417
so I wrote for two weeks
and it was unbelievable.

00:11:10.417 --> 00:11:13.153
- And in third grade Suzy
Thompson and I like--

00:11:13.153 --> 00:11:13.986
- No.

00:11:13.986 --> 00:11:16.209
But I was writing about my
family coming to this country,

00:11:16.209 --> 00:11:19.020
finding their way and all of a sudden

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:20.714
my losing a job paled in significance.

00:11:20.714 --> 00:11:22.330
- Seemed small, love that.

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:24.439
- And I know your family same thing.

00:11:24.439 --> 00:11:25.900
- [Gary] Perspective, it's powerful.

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:28.010
- And all the sudden I had
a different perspective

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:29.727
and I'm looking at this and I'm saying,

00:11:29.727 --> 00:11:31.840
"God, I gotta get over this."

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:32.750
- [Gary] Good for you.

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:34.640
- This is not the worst
thing that's happened

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.960
to my family in my history.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:37.810
- [Gary] Right yeah.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:40.960
- And so it really helped
me get some perspective

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:42.030
- [Gary] And then what happened?

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:43.520
- This is what was interesting.

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:45.829
He read the story and he got to know me

00:11:45.829 --> 00:11:49.107
and within another two
weeks when we met he said,

00:11:49.107 --> 00:11:51.480
"I don't get it, this
just doesn't add up."

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:52.313
And I said, "Why?"

00:11:52.313 --> 00:11:54.087
He said, "The person who wrote this story

00:11:54.087 --> 00:11:57.247
"is a fierce competitor and is determined

00:11:57.247 --> 00:11:58.637
"to make a difference.

00:11:58.637 --> 00:12:00.570
"And you're showing up--"

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:01.420
- [Gary] Tail between the legs.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:03.845
- Yeah "And you're not showing up,

00:12:03.845 --> 00:12:06.033
"the person I'm reading about

00:12:06.033 --> 00:12:08.970
"and the person I'm meeting
are two different people."

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:12.761
I'm an introvert, even
more then than I am now

00:12:12.761 --> 00:12:15.810
and I wasn't talking unless spoken to.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:17.577
I was very polite, I was trying to fit in,

00:12:17.577 --> 00:12:21.730
make it work, keep my job and do all this.

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:22.563
- [Gary] Defense.

00:12:22.563 --> 00:12:23.440
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:25.297
And I was reacting to things and he said,

00:12:25.297 --> 00:12:27.750
"You've got so much to offer,

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:30.626
"but you're keeping it
all bottled up inside."

00:12:30.626 --> 00:12:35.220
And that's where the spirit of
this book started to be born

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:38.050
because you know my observation

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:39.150
and we do a little story,

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:41.131
David Brooks from the New
York Times writes about,

00:12:41.131 --> 00:12:44.109
are you living for your
resume or your eulogy?

00:12:44.109 --> 00:12:48.129
Are you trying to get
all the tangible things,

00:12:48.129 --> 00:12:50.000
the new car,

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.927
the corner office, are you living for that

00:12:53.927 --> 00:12:56.140
or are you living for
what they're gonna say

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:58.040
about you when you're gone?

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:02.180
And he said, "You get seduced
living for your resume."

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:05.220
And I started realizing that
you got to put your resume

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:07.040
and your eulogy together

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:09.890
and it should be meant together
with a sense of purpose

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:12.094
that grows out of your life story

00:13:12.094 --> 00:13:15.440
not out of all the testing
and all this other stuff,

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:17.130
grows out of your life story

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:18.610
because as we see in the book,

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:20.662
your life story is your leadership story.

00:13:20.662 --> 00:13:22.190
It's where it comes from.

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:25.250
- I met with a young woman
that doesn't work here

00:13:25.250 --> 00:13:27.050
and she wants to go do something

00:13:27.050 --> 00:13:29.540
and she's not leaving
because she's worried

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:31.440
that her resume's gonna
show she only worked

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:33.024
at this place for six months.

00:13:33.024 --> 00:13:37.700
I was like it's 2020, nobody
even fucking reads resumes.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:39.160
What are you talking about?

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:40.110
- That's a good point.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:43.564
When I lost my job it's
like you were ashamed

00:13:43.564 --> 00:13:46.360
that you lost a job and
you didn't tell anybody.

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:48.294
- People still are that way.

00:13:48.294 --> 00:13:49.600
Stigma's are real.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:50.800
So what happened with your career?

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:52.190
Where did you get picked up?

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:54.920
- I ended up going to Kraft,
had a good run at Kraft,

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.529
where I met Peter and Carl.

00:13:57.529 --> 00:13:59.651
- Did you go to Chicago?

00:13:59.651 --> 00:14:01.440
- I went back to Chicago,

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:03.210
tail between my legs to a degree

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:04.257
because that's where I grew up.

00:14:04.257 --> 00:14:06.076
- Yeah, but Kraft was a job.

00:14:06.076 --> 00:14:07.610
- I had a great job there.

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.800
- What was the entry point, what brand?

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:14.780
- I was a category manager
for Kraft salad dressings,

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:18.996
but I went on to be director
of corporate strategy at Kraft

00:14:18.996 --> 00:14:21.307
and then I got recruited right after

00:14:21.307 --> 00:14:24.830
"Barbarians at the Gate"
by KKR, I got recruited--

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:25.670
- Tell everybody a little bit about,

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:27.730
this is an iconic book
in the corporate world.

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:29.550
I want some of the
youngsters and entrepreneurs

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:31.410
outside the corporate world to hear this.

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:32.920
Tell everybody just a few seconds

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:34.922
on that iconic book and story.

00:14:34.922 --> 00:14:38.300
- Well this was the story of

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:40.210
the second half of the 20th century,

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:42.260
it was the world's
largest LBO at the time,

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:45.265
it was KKR buying RJR Nabisco.

00:14:45.265 --> 00:14:49.229
RJR was a money machine, tobacco machine.

00:14:49.229 --> 00:14:54.229
They acquired Nabisco and
some other food companies

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:58.661
and built this massive business

00:14:58.661 --> 00:15:02.840
but the handwriting was on the
wall for the tobacco industry

00:15:04.981 --> 00:15:07.150
and so business started to soften.

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:11.380
KKR came in and made it
the world's largest LBO.

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:14.253
They planned to flip it in
five years, it took us 10.

00:15:17.651 --> 00:15:19.900
And there was so much money

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:25.640
coursing through the
veins of this company that

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.130
it was like the Wild West of

00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:31.558
corporate spending and this is--

00:15:31.558 --> 00:15:32.583
- Wasting.

00:15:32.583 --> 00:15:36.173
- Yeah, there were stories of
the chairman who since passed

00:15:36.173 --> 00:15:40.190
sending the corporate
jet to pick up his dog

00:15:40.190 --> 00:15:42.907
on the East Coast to come out
to Mission Hills, California

00:15:42.907 --> 00:15:45.840
for a golf tournament on the West coast.

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:47.560
- The dog?
- The dog.

00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:50.910
And we had a team.

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:52.600
He had assembled a team of

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:54.027
the greatest athletes of all time.

00:15:54.027 --> 00:15:55.652
- [Gary] Oh, I know this story.

00:15:55.652 --> 00:15:58.691
- The Johnny Unitas, all of them,

00:15:58.691 --> 00:16:01.960
they really had no income
in their retirement,

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:03.660
they hadn't made the money, right?

00:16:04.722 --> 00:16:08.850
They had about 30 people on payroll

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:13.250
who lived off RJR Nabisco
and showed up at events

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:14.410
and everything else.

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:17.380
It was a larger payroll I
believe than the Yankees

00:16:17.380 --> 00:16:21.640
at the time but it was the
who's who of Hall of Fame land--

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:23.070
- 'Cause he loved sports.

00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:24.130
And just wanted hang out with those guys?

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:25.390
- Yeah, hang out.

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:28.243
And you know, it worked.

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:32.273
It was just amazing excess.

00:16:32.273 --> 00:16:35.810
KKR bought it, we had a
sort of wind all that down

00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:38.490
because corporate governance
was getting in the way.

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:41.998
I mean you couldn't do
some of that stuff and so--

00:16:41.998 --> 00:16:42.831
- [Gary] Cleaned it up.

00:16:42.831 --> 00:16:44.487
- we ended up taking a lot of cost out.

00:16:44.487 --> 00:16:45.760
I had a good run there.

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:48.840
The last five years I was
president of Nabisco Foods

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:51.557
running that and then we sold that

00:16:51.557 --> 00:16:54.609
actually back to Kraft, who I had left

00:16:54.609 --> 00:16:58.320
and then I was recruited to go help

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:00.840
fix Campbell's Soup Company,

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:03.830
which I was there for a decade as CEO

00:17:05.461 --> 00:17:06.810
and that was wild.

00:17:06.810 --> 00:17:11.075
I went in there and
headquartered in Canada.

00:17:11.075 --> 00:17:12.189
- How did that feel?

00:17:12.189 --> 00:17:15.089
Remind me, I apologize,
was it directly to CEO?

00:17:15.089 --> 00:17:16.080
- Yeah.

00:17:16.080 --> 00:17:17.370
- So how did that feel?

00:17:17.370 --> 00:17:19.137
How did it feel on the dawn of knowing

00:17:19.137 --> 00:17:22.190
you're getting offered the CEO job

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:24.220
of one of the most iconic American,

00:17:24.220 --> 00:17:27.190
I mean this is now your
full career at this point

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:30.195
from the early days and Parker
Brothers and General Mills.

00:17:30.195 --> 00:17:31.947
- From you should be
looking for another job.

00:17:31.947 --> 00:17:34.887
You should be clean out
your desk, now I'm CEO.

00:17:34.887 --> 00:17:37.750
- Are you more like me, did
you try to find those two guys

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:39.243
that wrote that review the
night before you took--

00:17:39.243 --> 00:17:40.076
- I'm not you.

00:17:40.076 --> 00:17:41.539
- Yeah, I would have found those fuckers.

00:17:41.539 --> 00:17:42.437
(laughing)

00:17:42.437 --> 00:17:43.270
And be like, Hey!

00:17:43.270 --> 00:17:45.098
Ronald, I got something for you.

00:17:45.098 --> 00:17:46.880
- They know, they know.

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:48.880
- Of course they know, I know they know.

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:52.313
Was that a--

00:17:52.313 --> 00:17:54.310
- It was awesome.
- It was a head hunter?

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:55.653
Was it a head hunter?

00:17:56.920 --> 00:18:00.400
- Yeah, but we had been acquired

00:18:02.224 --> 00:18:05.960
and the market knew I was
not gonna go back to Kraft.

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:08.440
So there were several
opportunities percolating.

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:10.013
I ended up taking the Campbell's one.

00:18:10.013 --> 00:18:11.630
- Were they all CEO jobs or?

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:12.463
- Yeah.
- Got it.

00:18:12.463 --> 00:18:13.970
- That's the only one I would've done.

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:15.240
- Go to at that point in your career.

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:16.131
I get it.

00:18:16.131 --> 00:18:16.964
Was it exciting?

00:18:16.964 --> 00:18:17.797
- I always said you know,

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:20.943
I'm slow so I'm not as fast as you

00:18:20.943 --> 00:18:24.830
but I had always said I want
to be a CEO by the time I'm 49

00:18:26.489 --> 00:18:28.920
and I was 49 when it happened.

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:30.070
- This you'll find funny, Doug.

00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:33.240
I speak fast, my energy's fast,

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:34.960
but I'm actually wildly slow

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:37.024
like even this company I'm running now.

00:18:37.024 --> 00:18:39.530
When we met way back when for some org,

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:42.020
this has been built really to buy brands.

00:18:42.020 --> 00:18:45.340
I'm far more KKR, far more private equity,

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:48.160
than I am agency like
I'm actually going slow.

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:49.590
The energy's high.

00:18:49.590 --> 00:18:53.168
I would argue 49's a pretty
young age at that time of year

00:18:53.168 --> 00:18:56.211
of that era to be the CEO of
something like Campbell's.

00:18:56.211 --> 00:18:57.980
It was fast.
- It was.

00:18:57.980 --> 00:18:59.580
- It's pretty cool.
- Yeah, well it was.

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:02.460
- What was the biggest challenge
with that kind of family,

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:04.120
there's a lot of challenges going,

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:05.830
I happen to have enough knowledge to know

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:07.280
like the Campbell's family died,

00:19:07.280 --> 00:19:08.440
like there's some dynamics there.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:09.800
- The family owned half the company

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:11.020
and they were five families

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:12.460
because it was third generation.

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:13.996
You know, multiple generations.

00:19:13.996 --> 00:19:15.270
- [Gary] That's right, tricky.

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:16.880
- And we were headquartered in the poorest

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:18.570
most dangerous city in the United States

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:19.510
Camden, New Jersey.

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:20.343
- [Gary] Challenge.

00:19:20.343 --> 00:19:23.003
- 75,000 people, 70 murders a year.

00:19:24.612 --> 00:19:27.899
And we were running, it
was a canned soup company.

00:19:27.899 --> 00:19:32.319
I mean our product had been
invented in the prior century

00:19:32.319 --> 00:19:35.200
and was unchanged and I was saying,

00:19:35.200 --> 00:19:37.894
I was talking to our folks and I said,

00:19:37.894 --> 00:19:39.730
"Don't we need to evolve?"

00:19:39.730 --> 00:19:40.977
And they said, "You don't understand,

00:19:40.977 --> 00:19:43.957
"we can run canned soup
faster on our lines

00:19:43.957 --> 00:19:45.670
"than anybody else."

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:49.129
And I said, "But don't we need more?"

00:19:49.129 --> 00:19:53.367
And they said, "But we run
our canned soups really fast."

00:19:54.502 --> 00:19:57.310
And so we evolved but the company--

00:19:57.310 --> 00:19:58.840
- What's the biggest
highlight for you personally

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:00.110
in that decade?

00:20:00.110 --> 00:20:03.300
If I'm saying to you in that
10-year window, hot take--

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:07.525
- You know we talked earlier
about your resume or eulogy,

00:20:07.525 --> 00:20:09.573
that came together for me.

00:20:09.573 --> 00:20:12.413
I showed up more authentically--

00:20:12.413 --> 00:20:14.830
- During that era.
- In that era,

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:16.700
actually the last five years at Nabisco

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:19.183
I started to mature, I found my sea legs,

00:20:19.183 --> 00:20:21.800
I developed the courage of my convictions

00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:23.680
that I probably hadn't had before.

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:24.513
- [Gary] Love that.

00:20:24.513 --> 00:20:26.812
- But being challenged
by KKR all the time.

00:20:26.812 --> 00:20:27.856
