00:21:20.602 --> 00:21:22.270
She writes all the social,

00:21:22.270 --> 00:21:24.340
she's editor in chief
for ConantLeadership.

00:21:24.340 --> 00:21:26.532
She writes all of our
social media content.

00:21:26.532 --> 00:21:28.930
- And what is ConantLeadership
for everybody?

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:31.180
- Excuse me?
- What's ConantLeadership?

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:35.270
- It's what we run to promote leadership

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:38.700
that works in the 21st century
because most of the models--

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:39.860
- Is it a consulting business?

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:41.800
For everybody who's watching,
is it a consulting business?

00:21:41.800 --> 00:21:44.500
- We don't any consulting,
we don't make money,

00:21:44.500 --> 00:21:46.440
I don't take a salary.

00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:49.120
We charge for a few
things to cover our costs.

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:53.333
If we more than cover our
costs, we give it away and--

00:21:53.333 --> 00:21:54.540
- It's a legacy play.

00:21:54.540 --> 00:21:55.413
- And we've been doing this
now for like seven years.

00:21:55.413 --> 00:21:56.246
- Good for you.

00:21:56.246 --> 00:21:58.425
- And and we're loving it.

00:21:58.425 --> 00:21:59.477
We're in it for all the right reasons.

00:21:59.477 --> 00:22:00.970
- Is the book out?

00:22:00.970 --> 00:22:02.690
- The book just came out yesterday.

00:22:02.690 --> 00:22:03.523
- Congratulations.

00:22:03.523 --> 00:22:05.405
Who do you feel like this is gonna hit

00:22:05.405 --> 00:22:08.022
most right in the kind of chest?

00:22:08.022 --> 00:22:13.022
- This can hit anybody from
my kids who are 33, 35, and 38

00:22:13.842 --> 00:22:18.816
on up and that could actually
work with college students.

00:22:18.816 --> 00:22:20.512
The beauty of this book is

00:22:20.512 --> 00:22:23.743
there is no one right way to lead.

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:26.130
And if I talk about

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:28.560
your leadership story is your life story,

00:22:28.560 --> 00:22:31.070
your life story is your
leadership story, excuse me,

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:32.630
your life story's unique.

00:22:32.630 --> 00:22:34.100
- Snowflake.
- And you should lead

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:35.040
in a unique way.

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:37.850
There are some timeless
principles that you should apply,

00:22:37.850 --> 00:22:39.420
which we talk about in the book

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:41.080
but this book is designed for you

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:43.538
to create your own blueprint.

00:22:43.538 --> 00:22:47.321
You, anybody in this room
and it will all be different.

00:22:47.321 --> 00:22:51.656
I have probably, because I'm older,

00:22:51.656 --> 00:22:53.381
I have thousands of books.

00:22:53.381 --> 00:22:56.008
Probably over a thousand
books in the office

00:22:56.008 --> 00:22:59.330
and there are a thousand
books that tell you

00:22:59.330 --> 00:23:00.660
how you oughta lead

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:03.030
and none of them resonate
with me completely.

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:05.240
They're good, a good idea
here, good idea there.

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:08.060
So what we decided to do was write a book

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:09.938
that help people figure out how to lead

00:23:09.938 --> 00:23:12.340
in a way that worked for them.

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:15.650
- How much is relationship with adversity

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:18.726
and losing self esteem, self-awareness,

00:23:18.726 --> 00:23:20.232
how many of these--

00:23:20.232 --> 00:23:22.950
- I mean, it's all part of it.

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:24.090
This is complicated.

00:23:24.090 --> 00:23:25.660
If it was not complicated--

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:26.760
- [Gary] Everybody would do it.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:28.225
- It would have been figured out already.

00:23:28.225 --> 00:23:32.453
- And the key to this book
is and all those other books,

00:23:34.743 --> 00:23:37.471
it's like a diet after the holidays,

00:23:37.471 --> 00:23:40.009
you say I'm gonna lose 20 pounds

00:23:40.009 --> 00:23:43.070
and here we go, my wife and
I are gonna start a diet.

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:44.480
We last about two weeks and we say,

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:46.772
this is never gonna work,
it doesn't fit in our life.

00:23:46.772 --> 00:23:49.423
And the way we wrote this book was

00:23:49.423 --> 00:23:51.480
it has to nest perfectly,

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:54.780
the change process, perfectly
in your cockamamie life,

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:56.320
the way it exists today.

00:23:56.320 --> 00:23:58.780
Anybody in your office, all of whom,

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:00.690
everybody, feels swamped.

00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:01.841
- 100%.
- Swamped, right?

00:24:01.841 --> 00:24:02.780
- It's unbelievable.

00:24:02.780 --> 00:24:03.613
- And they say how do I fit this in?

00:24:03.613 --> 00:24:05.730
- I watch people who have
one hours worth of work

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:06.563
feel like they're swamped

00:24:06.563 --> 00:24:07.769
and I want to kick them in the face.

00:24:07.769 --> 00:24:08.602
(laughing)

00:24:08.602 --> 00:24:09.650
No, it's true it's the human spirit.

00:24:09.650 --> 00:24:11.397
People fill their bucket with--

00:24:11.397 --> 00:24:12.330
- [Dough] With stuff.

00:24:12.330 --> 00:24:13.530
- Let me make fun of myself.

00:24:13.530 --> 00:24:16.620
I thought I was the
hardest-working guy going at 27,

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:17.673
I laugh at that kid.

00:24:17.673 --> 00:24:19.100
- [Doug] Well you were
pretty hard working.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:21.250
- I was.
- I knew you then. (laughing)

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:23.350
- But really you know
you hit different gears,

00:24:23.350 --> 00:24:24.610
hit in strides, right?
- That's true.

00:24:24.610 --> 00:24:25.660
- It is around happiness

00:24:25.660 --> 00:24:26.640
or what you're supposed to be doing.

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:27.670
- Amy, jump in here for a second,

00:24:27.670 --> 00:24:29.327
I just wanna ask, go
ahead, don't be scared.

00:24:29.327 --> 00:24:30.740
I just wanna you a quick question.

00:24:30.740 --> 00:24:31.620
From your perspective

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:33.610
'cause I think it really
matters and gonna help,

00:24:33.610 --> 00:24:35.420
you know from your perspective

00:24:35.420 --> 00:24:37.700
as this book was written
what stood out to you?

00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:39.190
What was an a-ha for you

00:24:39.190 --> 00:24:40.220
maybe going through the process,

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:41.660
which I think sometimes happens

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:44.330
or what was your ambition
or as you were listening

00:24:44.330 --> 00:24:46.964
and debating and conjuring up with Doug,

00:24:46.964 --> 00:24:49.312
when you now look at this
as a finished product

00:24:49.312 --> 00:24:51.530
where do you go with like oh,

00:24:51.530 --> 00:24:53.720
I'm really glad this
happened through the process

00:24:53.720 --> 00:24:57.830
or I'm excited to see this
group of people absorb this

00:24:57.830 --> 00:25:00.259
and I'm excited, like some
random thoughts on that front.

00:25:00.259 --> 00:25:04.240
- Yeah, so I think one of
the epiphanies was anchoring

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:06.551
this story in that moment
of Doug getting fired

00:25:06.551 --> 00:25:09.170
because it brings somebody
with such gravitas

00:25:09.170 --> 00:25:10.812
down to earth, makes them relatable

00:25:10.812 --> 00:25:12.912
and it really is the story of

00:25:12.912 --> 00:25:15.106
the worst day in somebody's life--

00:25:15.106 --> 00:25:16.680
- Becoming the best.
- How to get from that day

00:25:16.680 --> 00:25:18.280
and how do you get there

00:25:18.280 --> 00:25:19.700
and it's a practical guide,

00:25:19.700 --> 00:25:21.615
so when you look at a story that big

00:25:21.615 --> 00:25:24.285
it can feel too big to be approachable.

00:25:24.285 --> 00:25:27.700
So that's how, we also
had kind of the epiphany

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:29.390
to make it really practical.

00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:32.460
People that I talk to
my age also feel swamped

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:35.026
whether they only have an
hour of work or 70 hours.

00:25:35.026 --> 00:25:37.337
And they're saying I don't feel,

00:25:37.337 --> 00:25:39.180
I need to wait for the perfect conditions

00:25:39.180 --> 00:25:40.538
to get started on a change process.

00:25:40.538 --> 00:25:41.917
When am I gonna feel ready?

00:25:41.917 --> 00:25:43.500
You're never gonna feel ready.

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:45.150
So this is for you whenever.

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:47.630
- How much do you feel
that is people's inability

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:50.096
to have a relationship with
other people's judgment?

00:25:50.096 --> 00:25:52.360
- I'm sorry, say that one more time.

00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:53.619
I need to think about that.

00:25:53.619 --> 00:25:55.480
- Sure, well I think like to your point

00:25:55.480 --> 00:25:57.160
and I couldn't agree with you more,

00:25:57.160 --> 00:26:00.940
like people come up with
enormous amounts of counterpoints

00:26:00.940 --> 00:26:02.575
to why this is a time to do something

00:26:02.575 --> 00:26:04.720
that would make them
happier, more successful.

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:05.900
And so it's something

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:06.850
I've been thinking about my whole life.

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:08.940
I think one of the great
gifts that I was given is

00:26:08.940 --> 00:26:11.120
immigrant, grew up with very little,

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:13.138
and then on top of it which
is rare for immigrants,

00:26:13.138 --> 00:26:14.763
bad student, right?

00:26:14.763 --> 00:26:16.460
That's an added element,

00:26:16.460 --> 00:26:19.026
which very early on gave me
a relationship with losing

00:26:19.026 --> 00:26:22.124
and being an outsider that
made me very comfortable

00:26:22.124 --> 00:26:24.796
with not worrying about people's judgment

00:26:24.796 --> 00:26:27.390
and I observe and I'm like right,

00:26:27.390 --> 00:26:29.280
they're scared to quit their good job

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:30.320
because they're unhappy

00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:32.100
because they're worried
about their mom's judgment,

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:34.435
their husband's judgment,
their best friend's judgment.

00:26:34.435 --> 00:26:37.300
In what you just said how
much have you observed

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:39.330
or what's your hypothesis
on how much of this

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:41.777
is actually grounded in people's inability

00:26:41.777 --> 00:26:44.490
to deal with other people's judgment?

00:26:44.490 --> 00:26:45.910
- Yeah, that's a really good point

00:26:45.910 --> 00:26:47.860
and you're actually lucky
that you failed early

00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:49.190
because you got comfortable with it.

00:26:49.190 --> 00:26:50.300
- Agreed.
- There's a lot of people

00:26:50.300 --> 00:26:52.910
especially who ascend to
the levels of leadership,

00:26:52.910 --> 00:26:54.560
the leaders Doug's talking to,

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:55.990
where they're used to being really type A

00:26:55.990 --> 00:26:57.954
and high achieving and
they're not comfortable

00:26:57.954 --> 00:27:00.012
with something that's imperfect.

00:27:00.012 --> 00:27:03.110
So the message in this
book is forget perfection,

00:27:03.110 --> 00:27:04.314
throw it out the window.

00:27:04.314 --> 00:27:05.660
- I hate perfection.
- It's not gonna be perfect.

00:27:05.660 --> 00:27:06.930
You just have to get going.

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:09.468
- Perfection is a disguise
for not wanting to do.

00:27:09.468 --> 00:27:13.940
- Absolutely, it becomes an
excuse to just not even start.

00:27:13.940 --> 00:27:15.340
100%, yeah.

00:27:15.340 --> 00:27:16.177
- [Gary] I love that.

00:27:16.177 --> 00:27:19.127
- And what Amy's
engineered in here is this

00:27:19.127 --> 00:27:21.840
you can start right
now with what you have,

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:23.416
with what you've got right away

00:27:23.416 --> 00:27:27.740
and within a week you can
start to behave in a way

00:27:27.740 --> 00:27:28.573
that works for you.
- Is there any questions?

00:27:28.573 --> 00:27:30.230
Let's get one question before we wrap up

00:27:30.230 --> 00:27:31.500
and then I'll give you the floor

00:27:31.500 --> 00:27:34.280
to give some thought to the
last things we want to say

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:35.861
before we head out of here.

00:27:35.861 --> 00:27:37.515
Yeah, it just makes sense.

00:27:37.515 --> 00:27:39.210
You know it's really funny.

00:27:39.210 --> 00:27:40.675
Podcast form, book form,

00:27:40.675 --> 00:27:44.260
you know ultimately I think
why I wasn't a good student

00:27:44.260 --> 00:27:45.250
is my relationship with reading.

00:27:45.250 --> 00:27:47.400
I was never diagnosed
but it's clear to me.

00:27:48.610 --> 00:27:50.392
I love that people learn
in different formats.

00:27:50.392 --> 00:27:51.225
- [Amy] Yes.

00:27:51.225 --> 00:27:52.340
- I love that.
(phone ringing)

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:53.436
- Who's this?
- Adam.

00:27:53.436 --> 00:27:54.269
- Adam.

00:27:57.003 --> 00:27:59.963
(phone ringing)

00:27:59.963 --> 00:28:02.480
Adam, don't blow your only chance.

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:04.505
Here comes one more, Adam.

00:28:04.505 --> 00:28:08.407
I hope we get a voice
message so I can leave one.

00:28:08.407 --> 00:28:10.110
- [Adam] Hello, this is Adam.

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:12.553
- Adam, this is Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:28:12.553 --> 00:28:14.348
(Adam laughing)

00:28:14.348 --> 00:28:15.795
- [Adam] How's it going?

00:28:15.795 --> 00:28:17.752
- It's going quite well,
say hello to Doug and Amy.

00:28:17.752 --> 00:28:19.350
- Hey Adam, what's going on?

00:28:19.350 --> 00:28:20.795
- [Adam] Hey Doug, hey Amy.

00:28:20.795 --> 00:28:22.870
It's going pretty well, how are you?

00:28:22.870 --> 00:28:24.843
- [Gary] Good, what's
your question brother?

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:28.653
- [Adam] You know I just I know,

00:28:28.653 --> 00:28:30.050
I have done some research in the past.

00:28:30.050 --> 00:28:32.210
I'm actually writing a blog right now.

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:33.810
It's kind of funny that I'm even on this

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:35.520
but I'm ready to blog right now

00:28:35.520 --> 00:28:39.280
and it's about just talking
about employees and clients

00:28:39.280 --> 00:28:40.610
and just showing appreciation

00:28:40.610 --> 00:28:43.929
and specifically how to write
the perfect thank you note

00:28:43.929 --> 00:28:47.252
and I know a little bit of Doug's history

00:28:47.252 --> 00:28:52.252
and how he has written I believe
over 30,000 thank you notes

00:28:52.951 --> 00:28:55.780
and I just kind of
wanted to pick his brain

00:28:55.780 --> 00:28:58.718
as far as like the impact
of just simply showing

00:28:58.718 --> 00:29:01.050
appreciation and being genuine

00:29:01.050 --> 00:29:03.140
and like what that means
and how do you do that

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:05.680
and any suggestions with that and yeah.

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:07.670
- Hey, well go to ConantLeadership.com,

00:29:07.670 --> 00:29:09.495
and we'll help you even more but--

00:29:09.495 --> 00:29:11.460
- Oh! (imitating cymbals)

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:12.510
- But lemme tell you.

00:29:13.778 --> 00:29:14.903
- Doug, hold on.

00:29:14.903 --> 00:29:16.840
First, you have to thank this guy

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:18.580
for knowing that history about you.

00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:21.030
- Well everybody, I'm
surprised you don't Gary.

00:29:21.030 --> 00:29:23.702
- You know what I did know
about the thank-you notes.

00:29:23.702 --> 00:29:26.020
Is the number in the range?

00:29:26.020 --> 00:29:27.060
- Nah, he's right.

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:29.200
- I did know big time
on the thank-you notes,

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:32.160
I didn't know, I mean that is scale.

00:29:32.160 --> 00:29:34.620
- Well you know and Gary, thank you,

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:36.320
when Adam, thank you.

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:40.349
When I job, my outplacement
counselor said,

00:29:40.349 --> 00:29:42.087
"You're gonna be a horrible interviewer.

00:29:42.087 --> 00:29:44.457
"You're too shy, you're too introverted.

00:29:44.457 --> 00:29:46.909
"You got to figure out a way
to have a signature practice

00:29:46.909 --> 00:29:50.630
"to get a job", and he
said, "Figure it out."

00:29:50.630 --> 00:29:54.430
So I started, I'd come
here, I'd meet 10 people.

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:57.431
I'd get all their names
while I was interviewing.

00:29:57.431 --> 00:29:59.740
I would go to the coffee shop next door.

00:29:59.740 --> 00:30:01.375
I would hand write a note to each of them.

00:30:01.375 --> 00:30:05.440
I would then walk it
back to the front desk,

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:06.987
give it to the receptionist

00:30:06.987 --> 00:30:09.490
and say could you deliver these today?

00:30:09.490 --> 00:30:11.070
I did that everywhere I went.

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:11.903
- [Gary] Wow.

00:30:11.903 --> 00:30:14.213
- And I started the
practice of reaching out

00:30:14.213 --> 00:30:16.312
in a personal way, it
doesn't have to be much.

00:30:16.312 --> 00:30:19.640
Receptionist had never received
a personal thank you note

00:30:19.640 --> 00:30:22.510
in their life from somebody
calling on the building.

00:30:22.510 --> 00:30:23.343
- [Gary] That's amazing.

00:30:23.343 --> 00:30:24.640
- And so I started doing that

00:30:24.640 --> 00:30:26.325
and then I went to work at Campbell

00:30:26.325 --> 00:30:29.284
and even in Campbell,
which was hemorrhaging,

00:30:29.284 --> 00:30:31.691
eight out of 10 things
being done we're right.

00:30:31.691 --> 00:30:33.800
We had to make some real tough decisions,

00:30:33.800 --> 00:30:36.275
let go 300 of the top 350 leaders.

00:30:36.275 --> 00:30:38.867
Never been done in Fortune 500 history.

00:30:38.867 --> 00:30:42.720
At the same time we had all
these people doing good work

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:45.230
so I started writing 10 to 20 short notes

00:30:45.230 --> 00:30:47.300
a day to employees,

00:30:47.300 --> 00:30:49.309
I was commuting two and
a half hours each way

00:30:49.309 --> 00:30:51.600
from New Jersey down to Camden,

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:53.800
from Morris County down to Camden.

00:30:53.800 --> 00:30:56.910
I started reading all the
stuff that was going on

00:30:56.910 --> 00:30:59.630
in the company and I would
write notes to these folks,

00:30:59.630 --> 00:31:02.428
10 to 20 a day, six days
a week for a decade.

00:31:02.428 --> 00:31:06.287
When I was retiring the
person from Forbes said,

00:31:06.287 --> 00:31:08.477
"How many of these, I'm
hearing about all these notes

00:31:08.477 --> 00:31:10.530
"from everybody, how many
notes have you written?"

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:11.910
I said, "I don't know."

00:31:11.910 --> 00:31:14.830
So we did the math, during
the interview we did the math

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:17.150
and figured out just to Campbell employees

00:31:17.150 --> 00:31:19.100
I had written 30,000 notes.

00:31:19.100 --> 00:31:21.016
We only had 20,000 employees.

00:31:21.016 --> 00:31:22.600
Wherever you went in the world,

00:31:22.600 --> 00:31:23.970
we were in 38 countries,

00:31:23.970 --> 00:31:25.684
we were marketed in 125 countries.

00:31:25.684 --> 00:31:28.750
Wherever you went in
the world in a cubicle

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:30.730
if I was to walk, you would see a note

00:31:30.730 --> 00:31:33.273
handwritten note from me--
- That's amazing.

00:31:33.273 --> 00:31:36.170
- In just about every
person's cubicle somewhere.

00:31:36.170 --> 00:31:37.450
- You know what's amazing about that?

00:31:37.450 --> 00:31:40.000
Adam, any parting shots
'cause I'm gonna wrap up here

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:41.432
but thank you for that.

00:31:41.432 --> 00:31:43.000
- [Adam] That's awesome.

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:45.532
- Adam, make it personal
and keep it short.

00:31:45.532 --> 00:31:47.750
- [Adam] Awesome, thank you so much.

00:31:47.750 --> 00:31:48.780
- You got it brother.

00:31:48.780 --> 00:31:50.430
It's funny, a lot of
people don't know this

00:31:50.430 --> 00:31:52.160
but in my transition
from the wine business

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:53.380
to the tech world,

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:58.380
I reply to every single tweet
from 2007 to 2011, period.

00:32:00.080 --> 00:32:02.998
Anybody who replied to me and I think--

00:32:02.998 --> 00:32:03.977
- I can only, yeah.

00:32:03.977 --> 00:32:06.400
- But it's the same game you played.

00:32:06.400 --> 00:32:08.810
In makes, in context, right?

00:32:08.810 --> 00:32:09.643
- Before Twitter, yeah.

00:32:09.643 --> 00:32:11.200
- You know like sending a
note to Amy and being like,

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:12.890
I liked your black and white jacket

00:32:12.890 --> 00:32:13.860
that you wore in the office today.

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:16.070
Like when you actually
are paying attention,

00:32:16.070 --> 00:32:17.394
when you actually know a little something,

00:32:17.394 --> 00:32:19.433
it's subtle little things that really,

00:32:19.433 --> 00:32:20.750
it's listening.

00:32:20.750 --> 00:32:22.380
- I gotta tell you though,

00:32:22.380 --> 00:32:24.960
the notes I wrote we're not
gratuitous, I like your jacket.

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:28.380
They were, you delivered the
business on plan under budget

00:32:28.380 --> 00:32:31.728
or nice job with the diversity network.

00:32:31.728 --> 00:32:35.545
Every note I sent was
reaffirming our company strategy

00:32:35.545 --> 00:32:37.880
and I was talking performance,

00:32:37.880 --> 00:32:39.760
but you know the theme was

00:32:39.760 --> 00:32:41.510
I'm gonna be tough minded on standards.

00:32:41.510 --> 00:32:42.950
We let a lot of people go

00:32:42.950 --> 00:32:44.821
but I'm gonna be tender
hearted with people.

00:32:44.821 --> 00:32:46.780
And the people that were
getting the work done,

00:32:46.780 --> 00:32:48.120
they heard from me too.

00:32:48.120 --> 00:32:48.953
- [Gary] I love that.

00:32:48.953 --> 00:32:50.880
- So this is not about--

00:32:50.880 --> 00:32:52.210
- Mine would've been like Amy, good job.

00:32:52.210 --> 00:32:54.380
We got the pages in and
I also like your jacket.

00:32:54.380 --> 00:32:56.180
- Well--
- I'm kidding, I'm kidding.

00:32:56.180 --> 00:32:58.560
- I wanted no mistakes.
- I respect that,

00:32:58.560 --> 00:32:59.393
no, I respect that.

00:32:59.393 --> 00:33:00.600
- You worry that people think oh,

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:02.778
we're just gonna hold
hands and sing Kumbaya.

00:33:02.778 --> 00:33:03.950
That's not it.
- By the way,

00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:05.490
it's back to "The Blueprint."

00:33:05.490 --> 00:33:07.100
It's back to 11,000 books.

00:33:07.100 --> 00:33:09.470
It's I would done that and
you would have done this

00:33:09.470 --> 00:33:10.510
and that's the biggest punchline.

00:33:10.510 --> 00:33:13.257
The biggest fear I have right
now is in my popularity growth

00:33:13.257 --> 00:33:14.740
that people gonna try to be like me.

00:33:14.740 --> 00:33:16.020
I'm an enigma.

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:17.530
I was in outer space.

00:33:17.530 --> 00:33:19.093
I grew up in a very different,

00:33:20.047 --> 00:33:22.000
I was born in the old country.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:23.907
I'm more similar to
everybody's grandfather

00:33:23.907 --> 00:33:25.340
than I am to them.

00:33:25.340 --> 00:33:26.540
I'm a different animal.

00:33:26.540 --> 00:33:28.755
Don't do it like me, don't do it like you,

00:33:28.755 --> 00:33:32.470
do it like us in our
buying into who we were

00:33:32.470 --> 00:33:33.900
and how we could do it.

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:36.781
- Yeah, it's your story, you write it.

00:33:36.781 --> 00:33:38.660
- 100%.
- It's not our story.

00:33:38.660 --> 00:33:41.670
And all these leadership
books are here's my story

00:33:41.670 --> 00:33:42.960
and here's how you should do it.

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:44.033
- It's wrong.
- Forget about that.

00:33:44.033 --> 00:33:45.077
- I'm with you, it's wrong.

00:33:45.077 --> 00:33:45.910
- It's the wrong paradigm.

00:33:45.910 --> 00:33:46.930
- Now if you're the kind of learner

00:33:46.930 --> 00:33:48.500
that learns from other people's examples,

00:33:48.500 --> 00:33:49.603
that's why you went on to read those books

00:33:49.603 --> 00:33:51.880
because you were able
to get a little here,

00:33:51.880 --> 00:33:54.180
a little there and at 15, 20 bucks a pop

00:33:54.180 --> 00:33:56.540
and if you're a quick
reader, you can get value.

00:33:56.540 --> 00:33:57.440
It's a frame work.

00:33:57.440 --> 00:33:59.383
- There are people you can learn from.

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:04.143
I met Stephen Covey when
he was Stephen Covey.

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:09.450
He was advising presidents and I and he,

00:34:09.450 --> 00:34:12.110
you know when the student is
ready, the teacher appears.

00:34:12.110 --> 00:34:14.310
And he had a profound influence on me,

00:34:14.310 --> 00:34:17.234
so did Neil McKenna who was
my outplacement counselor.

00:34:17.234 --> 00:34:18.745
Right time, right place.

00:34:18.745 --> 00:34:19.740
- [Gary] Right human.

00:34:19.740 --> 00:34:21.407
- I mean there are people to learn from

00:34:21.407 --> 00:34:23.629
but you have to listen
to your inner voice.

00:34:23.629 --> 00:34:24.462
- [Gary] Love.

00:34:24.462 --> 00:34:26.442
- And our observation is most people

00:34:26.442 --> 00:34:28.570
don't know their inner voice.

00:34:28.570 --> 00:34:30.350
They haven't reflected on it--

00:34:30.350 --> 00:34:32.660
- Or they suppress it or they fear it

00:34:32.660 --> 00:34:34.287
'cause it doesn't fit in

00:34:34.287 --> 00:34:36.260
or 'cause they fear it
because they hear it

00:34:36.260 --> 00:34:38.698
but they don't see it
fitting into the narrative

00:34:38.698 --> 00:34:40.280
that has been forced on them.

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:42.170
- Right, well said.

00:34:42.170 --> 00:34:43.824
We could have used when we wrote the book.

00:34:43.824 --> 00:34:44.760
(laughing)

00:34:44.760 --> 00:34:46.120
Where were you when I needed you?

00:34:46.120 --> 00:34:47.360
- I'm here right now when it matters.

00:34:47.360 --> 00:34:48.940
Watch this, everybody who's watching,

00:34:48.940 --> 00:34:50.590
go to Amazon or your local book store,

00:34:50.590 --> 00:34:52.850
BarnesandNobles.com and
pick up "The Blueprint."

00:34:52.850 --> 00:34:54.300
Doug, Amy, thank you so much.

